<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David IV of Georgia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1073–<a href="/years/1125">1125</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as David the Builder</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, known as the Builder, became king in 1089 and stopped paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. He reformed the army and the church, annexed Kakheti-Hereti and Lori, and defeated a large Seljuk coalition at Didgori in 1121. In 1122 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> and made it his capital, opening Georgia's golden age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia.jpg" alt="David IV of Georgia" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Certain types of Egyptian works (official documents, stamps, works of national folklore) should use specific license ... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_IV,_detail_from_icon,_St._Catherine_Monastery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
